<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_shock::shipping_security_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalations in China–West rivalry increase naval patrols, harassment of commercial traffic, and use of gray‑zone tactics in chokepoints (e.g., South China Sea, Malacca), directly raising the incidence and severity of threats to maritime energy logistics.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_shock::crude_oil_price" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened geopolitical tension drives a supply risk premium, triggers sanctions and precautionary buying, and disrupts contracted flows, which directly push up aggregate crude oil prices.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="monetary_policy_shock::sovereign_spread_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating China–West political and geoeconomic tensions raise perceived country and policy risk for states tied to either bloc, prompting investors to demand higher risk premia and widening sovereign bond spreads.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">monetary_policy_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="russia_ukraine_war::diplomatic_cohesion_leve" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising China–West tensions force some states to recalibrate trade and political ties with China, fracturing unanimity in multilateral forums and weakening coordinated diplomatic pressure and support streams related to the Russia–Ukraine conflict.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="us_iran_gulf_conflict::shipping_risk_perception" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising China–West strategic friction drives more naval presence, shadowing, and aggressive signaling in key sea lanes, causing carriers, operators and insurers to reassess and raise perceived maritime transit risk in the Gulf theater.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
